# {{ cluster_name }} development

Infrastructure as code for one Proxmox + ZFS + k3s cluster, generated from
weisssrv-cluster-template. This skill is the operating map: it encodes the
workflow and the invariants, and points at the documents that hold everything
else. It does not restate them — open the file it names. Paths are
repository-relative, except the `references/` pages below, which are relative to
this skill's own directory.

## Reference files

Open the one the decision tree names rather than reading them all.

| File | Covers |
|---|---|
| `references/add-k8s-app.md` | the application checklist, alert rules, storage, scheduling, SSO |
| `references/add-vm-app.md` | Proxmox guest mechanics: inventory, zvols, firewall, deploy path |
| `references/debugging.md` | symptom to entry point, and the local gates that reproduce a CI failure |
| `references/maintenance-upgrades.md` | the version-bump and library-pin flows, patching and reboots |
| `references/cluster-access.md` | kubeconfig, secret references, SSH, the local toolchain |

## Template first

This repository is one cluster, not the product. Site data stays here:
inventory values, addresses, the application estate, this cluster's documents.
Anything generic goes upstream first and arrives here as a pin bump or a
`copier update` — a generic change landed only here is drift that the next
update has to fight.

`README.md` is the top-level fact source, `docs/RUNBOOKS.md` holds this
cluster's day-two procedures, `docs/ci-pipeline.md` describes the pipeline, and
`task --list` owns the command set. Never cite a `task` name you have not seen
in `task --list`: nothing checks it for you, and inventing one is the failure
mode this skill exists to prevent.

## Invariants

Violating one of these breaks production or fails review.

- **Never push to the default branch.** Every change ships as a branch plus a
  merge request, including one-line fixes. Flux and the deploy jobs act on the
  default branch, so a direct push is an unreviewed deploy.
- **Keep feature branches local until the merge request is ready, and confirm
  before pushing.** Do not open a merge request unprompted.
- **No secrets in git.** Host tooling references
  `{{ secrets.secret_ref('<item>', '<field>') }}`;
  workloads read a Secret produced by an `ExternalSecret`. A new credential is
  added to the vault and documented, never inlined.
- **Flux owns all of `kubernetes/`.** No `kubectl apply -k` and no `helm upgrade`
  as ongoing operations. The only exceptions: `task flux:dev-apply -- <path>`
  (reverted on the next reconcile), the two bootstrap Secrets, and objects
  Ansible provisions outside `kubernetes/`.
- **Kubernetes manifests carry no site literals.** Domains, addresses and CIDRs
  come from the `cluster-config` ConfigMap as `${cluster_...}` substitutions;
  versions come from `cluster-versions`. Writing `{{ internal_domain }}` or an
  address into a manifest is a review rejection — it is the exact debt this
  repository was generated to avoid.
- **Versions are single-sourced** in `ansible/inventories/prod/group_vars/all.yml`.
  After changing a pin, run `task flux:sync-versions` and commit the regenerated
  ConfigMap in the same commit. Never hand-edit a generated file.
- **Ansible roles are not vendored.** There is no `ansible/roles/`. Playbooks
  address `weisssrv.infra.<role>` by FQCN at the ref pinned in
  `ansible/requirements.yml`. A role needing a change is changed in the library
  and pulled in by bumping that ref — not copied in here.
- **Never edit a vendored library copy in place.** `scripts/vendored-manifest.yml`
  lists every file copied from the library, and its `forked:` entries are the
  only ones allowed to differ — each with a reason and the library blob it was
  last reconciled against. Refreshing a copy is `task lib:sync`, then copy and
  review the diff. An edit in place reds the byte-identity gate, the next
  refresh reverts it, and `copier update` carries it forward as an undeclared
  fork. Fix the bug in the library instead and bump `lib_ref`.
- **Never automate ZFS pool creation or destruction.** Ansible sets properties,
  creates datasets and zvols, and mounts them. Pools are manual.
- **Every service is observed.** A change that adds a service is incomplete
  without logs, metrics, a down-or-stale alert, and a probe if users reach it
  directly.
- **Comments describe the current state, briefly, in the present tense.** Three
  content lines per block is the ceiling. A block guarding a trap that causes an
  outage may open `CRITICAL:` and run to eight; that marker is for outages, not
  for emphasis or a long file header. No dates, no merge-request numbers, no
  "used to", no prior values. Longer rationale goes in the nearest `README.md`
  or in `docs/RUNBOOKS.md`, not above the code.
- **The skill rides along with the change.** A change that alters a workflow, a
  gate, an invariant or a canonical-doc pointer updates `SKILL.md` and the
  matching `references/` file in the same merge request. CI checks only the
  backticked repository paths this skill cites (`scripts/check-skill-refs.py`);
  nothing validates its task names or its prose.
- **No AI attribution** anywhere: commits, code, comments, documentation, merge
  request text.

## Decision tree

| Changing | Read first | Then |
|---|---|---|
| A Kubernetes application | `references/add-k8s-app.md`, then the closest existing directory under `kubernetes/apps/` | mirror its shape: namespace, workload, storage, ExternalSecret, certificate, ingress route, NetworkPolicy, autoscaling policy |
| A host or guest | `references/add-vm-app.md`, `ansible/inventories/prod/` and the [role's README in the library]({{ links.lib_tree() }}{{ lib_ref }}/ansible_collections/weisssrv/infra/roles) | inventory first, playbook second; per-host facts go inline on the host entry in `hosts.yml` (there is no `host_vars/` unless you create one) |
| Storage: pools, datasets, exports | `ansible/inventories/prod/group_vars/nas.yml` | declare only what exists — Ansible never creates or destroys a pool; a NEW or empty pool goes through `docs/RUNBOOKS.md` § Storage bootstrap first; per-guest zvols are `vm_additional_disks` in `hosts.yml` |
| Terraform state (DNS, tailnet, SSO) | the module directory and its README | plan, review, apply; SSO objects are a supervised apply |
| A version pin or an upgrade | `references/maintenance-upgrades.md`, `docs/RUNBOOKS.md` § Upgrades | `task maintenance:check-versions`, then bump, `task flux:sync-versions`, commit both files |
| An incident | `references/debugging.md`, `docs/RUNBOOKS.md` § Where to look first, then the service's dashboard and logs | `task flux:status`, `task infra:verify`, `task k3s:status` |
| Cluster access or a credential | `references/cluster-access.md`, `docs/RUNBOOKS.md` § Rotating a secret | `task flux:rotate-secret -- <ns>/<name>`, or re-run the task that injects it host-side |
| An alert or a dashboard | `references/add-k8s-app.md` § Alert rules, `kubernetes/infrastructure/observability/README.md` | an app's rules are a standalone `PrometheusRule` in that app's directory, not an addition to the platform map; every rule carries a `runbook_url` anchoring a heading that exists in `docs/RUNBOOKS.md`; validate with `task lint:prometheus-config` |
| A platform (role / CI / Terraform-module) change | [weisssrv-lib]({{ links.lib_home() }}) | it changes upstream and this repo bumps `lib_ref`; never vendor a role here |

### Where the generic half of a change goes

| The change | Lands in |
|---|---|
| Site data: inventory values, the app estate, this cluster's docs | here |
| A role, a CI job template, a Terraform module, a vendored gate | weisssrv-lib, then bump `lib_ref` here |
| Repository shape: directory layout, a gate every cluster wants, a Taskfile pattern | weisssrv-cluster-template, then `copier update` |

## Pre-merge gates

Run from the repository root:

- `task lint` — the local mirror of the CI lint stage (every job in it except
  `deploy-coverage`, which needs the MR's merge base). It includes `tests/`
  (`lint:invariants`), which is what catches a duplicate address or vmid, an
  address outside the LAN, and an unresolvable `${placeholder}`. Run it for
  every change.
- `task flux:lint` — whenever `kubernetes/` changed. It renders every
  Kustomization, substitutes, fails on any unresolved `${...}`, and runs the
  cluster-invariant gates over that corpus exactly as the CI flux-lint job's
  `extra_validation` does: HPA/VPA conflicts, a scraped namespace its
  NetworkPolicies do not admit Prometheus into, an unscoped
  `ClusterSecretStore`, a PVC with no `storageClassName`, and `helm template`
  over the value-heavy releases.
- `task lint:netpol-parity` — the LAN fence, and the one gate that reads the
  manifests on disk rather than the rendered corpus, so it also covers
  `kubernetes/clusters/{{ cluster_name }}/flux-system/`. Every public-egress
  `except:` list must match a canonical reserved-CIDR set; a deliberately
  peer-less egress rule is declared in `scripts/netpol-except.yaml` with its
  reason. `task lint` runs it.
- `task flux:sync-versions` — whenever a version pin changed; commit both files.
- `task hosts:sync` — whenever `hosts.yml` changed; commit the generated file.
- `task lint:prometheus-config` — whenever alert or recording rules changed.
  `task lint` runs it, and it needs `promtool` and `amtool` on PATH; its
  preconditions name a missing tool rather than skipping.
- `task infra:check -- --limit <host>` — dry-run before deploying an Ansible
  change to a live host.
- `pre-commit install` once per clone wires the local backstop that mirrors the
  tool-light CI jobs. The hook set is `.pre-commit-config.yaml`.

The Taskfile is an `includes:` tree: `Taskfile.yml` holds the top-level tasks and
one file per namespace lives under `taskfiles/<ns>.yml`. Task names are
unchanged, so `flux:lint` is the task `lint:` in `taskfiles/flux.yml`, and a
cross-namespace call carries a leading colon (`:flux:sync-versions`).

## Review and merge

- Merge request pipelines run the path-filtered lint, validate, test and
  security gates. They do not deploy.
- **Commit subjects are Conventional Commits** — `type(scope): summary`, with
  `feat|fix|chore|docs|refactor|ci|test` as the types in use. Nothing enforces
  it; the history is the contract.
- **Run the review loop to convergence before raising the merge request**:
  self-review, then the AI reviewer and any requested reviewer, address every
  valid finding, and repeat until none remain.
- Resolve every valid review finding and re-run the gates before merging. Fold
  any available version bumps into the same revision rather than opening a
  separate one.
- Merge request descriptions state what was tested, in the past tense — not a
  checklist of intentions.
- **Serialize merges.** Merging a second change while the first one's deploy
  pipeline is still running cancels that deploy, and a manual deploy against a
  target whose CI job is in flight races it. Wait, then reconcile the pipeline
  against the live state.
- After merge: Flux reconciles `kubernetes/` within about a minute
  (`task flux:reconcile` forces it) and the deploy jobs run the Ansible side.
  Verify with `task flux:status`, `task infra:verify`, `task k3s:status`.

## Upstream

The platform this cluster is assembled from lives in
**[weisssrv-lib]({{ links.lib_home() }})**: the Ansible
collection, the CI templates, the Terraform modules and the helper scripts, all
pinned at `{{ lib_ref }}`. The generator is
**weisssrv-cluster-template**;
`.copier-answers.yml` records what this cluster was generated from, and
`copier update` pulls in template improvements. A fix that would benefit any
cluster belongs upstream, not here.

When a variable or a CI input is not in this repository, it is in the library:

- role behaviour and variables — that role's `README.md` under
  `ansible_collections/weisssrv/infra/roles/`
- the inventory-wide variables roles alias — the collection `README.md`
- CI template inputs — `docs/INCLUDE-CONTRACT.md`
- what a `lib_ref` bump can break — `docs/VERSIONING.md`
- which behaviour is a seam and which is the backend itself — `docs/EXTENSIBILITY.md`
